  "description": "Hamburger Budget\" blues?\nThis bike is the cure. Although it uses 7005 butted aluminum instead of\nX-Fusion tubing, the Diamond Lite 2 features frame geometry and rear suspension\npivot placement identical to its higher-priced, Diamond-series siblings.\nYou'll also enjoy 9-speed shifting, air shock rear suspension and Avid\nlinear-pull brakes, just like the Diamond Super Lite (but just a whisker\nless light-okay, a handful of whiskers)\n\nAnd the Diamond Lite 2 has something you can't get from those snooty,\nuptown rigs: Up to 100mm of front suspension travel from the coil/air\nRock Shox Air Judy SL fork. Hook that up with the Fuji's optional Twin\nTechnology linkage for 4.3 inches of rear travel and you'll be Queen (or\nKing) of Cross-Country Cushiness.\n\nKeep the Diamond Lite 2 the way it is forever or start dabbling with upgrades.\nThe frame and fork are disc brake compatible if your desires are more\nDiamond Lite 3ish than Diamond Super Lite. But leave the rear suspension\npivot alone. It's the same sealed bearing type as the top-of-the-line\nbikes, so your rear wheel always goes squoosh-squoosh, not wibble-wobble,\nover bumps.\n\nWith the money you save, treat yourself to a good pair of shoes that latch\nonto your Wellgo clipless pedals.\n.",
